## Frequently Asked Questions

## What qualifies for California Lemon Law?

A vehicle qualifies under California Lemon Law if it has a serious defect that affects its use, value, or safety, and the manufacturer has been unable to fix it after multiple repair attempts. The issue must have been reported while the car was under the original manufacturer’s warranty, and the defect must not be caused by misuse or neglect.

Common qualifying issues include engine problems, faulty transmissions, electrical malfunctions, and persistent safety concerns. If your car has been in the shop repeatedly for the same problem, it may qualify as a lemon.

## What is the time limit for the Lemon Law in California?

California law gives car owners four years to file a Lemon Law claim, starting from when they first noticed the defect. However, waiting too long can make the process harder, especially if records of repair attempts are incomplete or if the warranty expires.

It’s best to start the process as soon as it’s clear that the manufacturer isn’t fixing the issue, so you don’t lose your chance to get compensation.

## How hard is it to win a Lemon Law case?

Winning a Lemon Law case depends on having clear repair records, warranty coverage, and proof that the defect impacts the vehicle’s reliability or safety. Many manufacturers try to fight claims by blaming the owner, disputing repair records, or offering low settlements.

That’s why many car owners choose to work with a Lemon Law attorney who knows how to push back against these tactics. The stronger your documentation, the better your chances of getting a refund, replacement, or cash settlement.

## How do I invoke California Lemon Law?

The process starts by bringing your vehicle to an authorized repair center for documented repair attempts. If the problem persists after multiple visits, you’ll need to gather all repair orders, invoices, and communications with the dealer or manufacturer.

At that point, filing a claim with the help of a Lemon Law attorney can make the difference between a fair settlement and getting stuck with a defective car. Manufacturers often stall or deny claims, but legal representation helps move things forward. If your car qualifies, you could receive a refund, replacement, or financial settlement for your trouble.
